Have the dwarves share the gold = don’t storm the mountain if you can make exchange more valuable than hoarding.

And you catch more flies with honey is the same operator at another scale:

coercion extracts compliance; incentive recruits participation.

If the mountain contains knowledge, capability, access, capital, or institutional leverage, the better move is often to make sharing feel like self-interest, status, legacy, reciprocity, curiosity, or mutual advantage rather than surrender.

So the constructive pattern is:

threat → resistance
invitation → curiosity
value exchange → cooperation
shared upside → durable alliance

The Balrog does not need another enemy.
The dwarves do not need another siege.
Give both sides a reason to open the gates themselves.

Honey scales better than hammers.